       + sum(delta_g * d_ij * I(group_i = g)) + e_ij}

where \code{y_ij} is \code{log_norm_cov} for bin \code{j} of sample \code{i}, \code{d_ij} is
\code{distance_from_anchor}, \code{beta_0} and \code{beta_1} are the fixed intercept
and slope for the reference group, \code{u_0i} and \code{u_1i} are per-sample
random intercept and slope deviations, and \code{gamma_g}, \code{delta_g} are
fixed group main-effects and group-by-distance interaction terms.

The random-effects structure \code{(1 + distance_from_anchor | sample)}
allows each sample to have its own intercept and its own slope. This
properly represents the fact that different samples have different
overall coverage levels and possibly different local decay rates
even within the same group.
